This Yi proverb translates to 'There is a brother, then there is a younger brother; there is a father, then there is a son.'  

Let's break it down word by word:  
'ꃴ' means 'there is' or 'have';  
'ꑲꆀ' means 'only' or 'then';  
'ꑌ' means 'younger brother';  
'ꀿ' means 'father' or 'elder';  
'ꌺ' means 'son'.  

Example sentence: 'ꃴꐥꑲꆀꑌꐥ，ꀿꐥꑲꆀꌺꐥ，ꎔꃅꀿꃀꀨꎹ.'  
Its Chinese meaning is: 'There is a brother, then there is a younger brother; there is a father, then there is a son. You should take good care of your elders.'

The phrase 'ꃺꑌꏢꀋꏬ，ꃶꌐꃬꀋꐗ' translates into Chinese as 'The family members come, but they don't cook sour vegetable soup; the in-laws come, but they don't kill a chicken.'  

Let's break it down word by word:  
'ꃺꑌ' means 'family or relatives';  
'ꏢ' refers to 'sour vegetable soup';  
'ꏬ' means 'to cook';  
'ꀋ' is a negation marker, like 'not';  
'ꃶꌐ' means 'in-laws';  
'ꃬ' means 'chicken';  
'ꐗ' means 'to kill or roast'.  

For example: 'ꃺꑌꏢꀋꏬ，ꃶꌐꃬꀋꐗꄸ，ꋚꎫꃅꋭꆏꎫꃅ.'  
This sentence means: There is a saying like this: 'When family comes, don't cook sour vegetable soup; when in-laws come, don't kill a chicken. You should know what to cook.'

The Nuosu phrase 'ꈌꊿꉚꄉꊿꑝ，ꃬꊿꉚꄉꊾꄵ' translates to Chinese as 'The dog bites people when it sees them, and the chicken pecks people when it sees them.' Its extended meaning is 'to treat people differently, to judge someone based on their status.'  

Let's break it down word by word:  
'ꈌ' means 'dog';  
'ꊿ' means 'person';  
'ꉚ' means 'looks at';  
'ꑝ' means 'bite';  
'ꃬ' means 'chicken';  
'ꊾ' means 'peck';  
'ꄵ' means 'to peck'.  

Example sentence: 'ꈌꊿꉚꄉꊿꑝ，ꃬꊿꉚꄉꊾꄵ，ꋋꆏꉚꄸꄉꅽꏯꏈꌠꉬ.'  
This translates to Chinese as: 'The dog bites people when it sees them, the chicken pecks people when it sees them, he says that because he looks down on you.'

This Naxi saying 'ꆿꃀꇧꇨꌺꀋꋠ' means 'A tiger will not eat its own cub.'  

Let's break it down word by word:  
'ꆿꃀ' means 'tiger';  
'ꇨ' means 'dangerous' or 'fierce';  
'ꌺ' means 'cub' or 'child';  
'ꀋꋠ' means 'to eat'.  

So, the whole phrase literally means 'A fierce tiger does not eat its own child.'  

For example: 'ꆿꃀꇧꇨꌺꀋꋠ，ꊨꏦꀉꑳꏭꑠꃅꊨꑵꀋꉬ.'  
Its Chinese meaning is: 'A tiger will not eat its own cub, so you shouldn't talk like that to your child.'

The Naxi proverb 'ꑭꐨꑍꉆ，ꇇꊂꑍꀋꉆ' translates to 'Footprints may fade, but deeds remain forever.'  

Let's break it down word by word:  
'ꑭꐨ' means 'footprints';  
'ꑍ' means 'to disappear';  
'ꉆ' means 'to vanish or fade away';  
'ꇇꊂ' means 'deeds or achievements';  
'ꑍꀋꉆ' means 'will not disappear, will remain forever.'  

Here's an example sentence: 'ꆏꃅꋺꌤꈧꌠꆹꐥꍂꃅꀋꑍꌠꉬ，ꑭꐨꑍꉆ，ꇇꊂꑍꀋꉆ.'  
Its meaning in Chinese is: 'Your achievements will never be erased. Footprints may fade with time, but deeds will always remain.'

This Nuosu proverb 'ꀉꐔꇖꅒꁭꂥꊋꀋꄿ，ꑭꆹꇖꅒꁱꆽꊋꀋꄿ' means 'A daughter-in-law can't win against her in-laws, and a calf's leg can't beat a cow's leg.'  

Let me break it down for you:  
'ꀉꐔ' means 'a girl or a daughter-in-law';  
'ꇖꅒ' means 'to argue or to be polite';  
'ꁭꂥ' means 'in-laws';  
'ꑭꆹ' means 'a calf's leg';  
'ꁱꆽ' means 'a cow's leg';  
'ꀋꄿ' means 'can't win or can't beat.'  

There's no example sentence for this proverb right now, so just understanding the overall meaning is enough.

This Naxi saying 'ꅩꃅꅇꀋꎹ，ꌺꃅꐚꀋꎹ' translates to 'A good woman doesn't file a complaint, and a good man doesn't make enemies.'  

Let's break it down:  
'ꅩꃅ' means 'a good woman';  
'ꅇ' means 'words' or 'speech';  
'ꀋ' is a negation marker;  
'ꎹ' means 'to look for' or 'to seek';  
'ꌺꃅ' means 'a good man';  
'ꐚ' means 'to make an enemy';  
'ꀋ' again is a negation;  
'ꎹ' is 'to look for' or 'to seek'.  

An example sentence would be: 'ꅩꃅꅇꀋꎹ，ꌺꃅꐚꀋꎹ，ꑠꃅꇫꏈꎷꑵꀋꉬ.'  
This translates to: 'A good woman doesn't file a complaint, and a good man doesn't make enemies. You shouldn't say things like that carelessly.'

This Nuosu proverb translates to 'If a person is hardworking, they will become rich; if an animal eats salt, it will grow fat.'  

Let's break it down word by word:  
'ꃰꊿ' means 'person';  
'ꈪꑌ' means 'hardworking';  
'ꈍꄮ' means 'time' or 'when';  
'ꈀꂪꀕ' means 'rich';  
'ꎇꃀ' means 'animal';  
'ꋂ' means 'salt';  
'ꑌ' means 'to have';  
'ꈀꎔꀕ' means 'fat' or 'plump'.  

For example: 'ꈪꑌꑳꆀꍑ，ꃰꊿ Hogan ya mao, hu ni gao ya zhi, hu ni gao ya mao, hu ni gao ya zhi.'  

This sentence means: You need to be hardworking. A person who is hardworking will become rich, and an animal that eats salt will grow fat.

'ꅇꎹꅇꀋꊌ，ꁣꐆꌕꇇꊌ' translates to '告状无结果，反挨三耳光' in Chinese, which means 'If you file a complaint, it ends in nothing, and you even get three slaps.'  

Let's break it down:  
'ꅇ' means 'word' or 'speech';  
'ꎹ' means 'to look for';  
'ꊌ' means 'there is';  
'ꀋ' is used to negate;  
'ꌕ' means 'three'.  

Example sentence: 'ꅇꎹꅇꀋꊌ，ꁣꐆꌕꇇꊌ，ꑠꃅꌞꏯꅇꎹꎷꑵꀋꉬ.'  

This means: If you keep complaining, you'll get nothing done and even get three slaps. So don't keep complaining to others.

The Nuosu proverb 'ꈄꌠꂴꆽꐊ，ꂴꐊꂴꅐꁧ，ꏦꌠꆹꐊ，ꐊꁮꇈ' means 'The brave go to the front and come out first; the fearful follow behind and are eventually eliminated.'  

Let's break it down word by word:  
'ꈄ' means 'brave';  
'ꂴꆽꐊ' means 'in front';  
'ꅐ' means 'appear' or 'emerge';  
'ꏦ' means 'fear';  
'ꆹꐊ' means 'behind';  
'ꁮ' means 'disappear'.  

Here's an example sentence: 'ꆈꌠꇐꀳꑠꇬꐥ：ꈄꌠꂴꆽꐊ，ꂴꐊꂴꅐꁧ，ꏦꌠꆹꐊ，ꐊꁮꇈ.'  

Its meaning in Chinese is: 'There's a Nuosu proverb: The brave go to the front and come out first; the fearful follow behind and are eventually eliminated.'

The Yi proverb 'ꀀꌺꍗꀋꌸ，ꐋꐊꇀꄟꉜ；ꀀꃆꍗꀋꌸ。ꃥꑟꇀꄟꉜ' translates to Chinese as: 'Don't praise your own son, you'll know his character when he interacts with others; don't praise your own wife, you'll see how she treats guests.'  

Let's break it down word by word:  
'ꀀ' means 'self' or 'one's own';  
'ꌺ' means 'son';  
'ꍗ' means 'praise' or 'boast';  
'ꐋꐊ' means 'interacting with people';  
'ꃆ' means 'wife';  
'ꃥꑟ' means 'guests arriving'.  

For example: 'ꀀꌺꍗꀋꌸ，ꐋꐊꇀꄟꉜ；ꀀꃆꍗꀋꌸ，ꃥꑟꇀꄟꉜ', translates to: 'Don't praise your own son, you'll see his character when he interacts with others; don't praise your own wife, you'll see how she treats guests.'

This saying 'ꆺꑳꅪꐥꆏꌐꇁ，ꐷꈭꈌꃮꆏꃥꇁ' means 'When a daughter-in-law comes to visit, the dog in the courtyard starts barking, and the guest arrives.'  

Let's break it down word by word:  
'ꆺꑳ' means 'home' or 'family';  
'ꅪ' means 'daughter';  
'ꐥ' means 'has' or 'there is';  
'ꌐ' means 'in-law' or 'relative';  
'ꐷꈭ' means 'courtyard';  
'ꈌ' means 'dog';  
'ꃮ' means 'to bark' or 'to call';  
'ꃥ' means 'guest';  
'ꇁ' means 'to come' or 'to arrive'.  

Here's an example sentence: 'ꆺꑳꅪꐥꆏꌐꇁ，ꐷꈭꈌꃮꆏꃥꇁꄷ，ꈌꃮꎷꇈꄹꃥꇁꅊꇈꀐ.'  
This translates into Chinese as: 'When a daughter-in-law comes to visit, the dog in the courtyard starts barking, and the guest arrives. The dog kept barking — it was really the guest arriving.'

This Naxi proverb means 'If you don't know how to do something yourself, watch what your friend does. If you don't know how to speak, listen to what your friend says.'  

Let's break it down word by word:  
'ꀂꃅ' means 'to act or behave on your own';  
'ꇮ' means 'to know or be able to';  
'ꐋꀨ' means 'friend';  
'ꉉ' means 'to speak or express'.  

Here's an example sentence:  
'ꊨꏦꈍꃅꀕꌠꊨꀋꄅꑲꆏ，ꏢꁮꊿꐤꊼꇅꅲ，ꀒꋏꇐꀳꇬ“ꀂꃅꀋꇮꐋꀨꉜ，ꀂꉉꀋꇮꐋꀨꅲ”'  
Its Chinese meaning is: 'If you don't know how to do something, ask someone nearby. The elders say: 'If you don't know how to do it yourself, watch what your friend does. If you don't know how to speak, listen to what your friend says.''

The Nuosu proverb 'ꌤꌠꆏꒉꄎ，ꂮꌠꆏꋙꍈ' means 'Give water to the thirsty and food to the hungry' in Chinese.  

Let's break it down word by word:  
'ꌤꌠ' means 'the thirsty person';  
'ꒉ' means 'water';  
'ꄎ' means 'to drink';  
'ꂮꌠ' means 'the hungry person';  
'ꋙ' means 'food';  
'ꍈ' means 'to give to eat'.  

Example sentence: 'ꌤꌠꆏꒉꄎ，ꂮꌠꆏꋙꍈ，ꉪꑠꃅꃅꄡꑟ.'  
Its Chinese meaning is: 'Give water to the thirsty and food to the hungry, we should do this.'

This彝文 proverb translates to 'There is no horse with all its body covered in patterns, and there is no person who knows everything.'  

Let's break it down word by word:  
'ꃅꋓꀕ' means 'a patterned horse';  
'ꈴ' means 'all';  
'ꂷ' means 'one/ a';  
'ꀋꐥ' means 'no';  
'ꃰꊿ' means 'person';  
'ꌧ' means 'knowledge or learning'.  

Here's an example sentence:  
'ꀋꌧꌠꊝꑲꆏꌞꊿꅲꀋꀬꃅꄡꐛ，ꃅꋓꀕꈴꂷꀋꐥ，ꃰꊿꌧꈴꂷꀋꐥ.'  

Its Chinese meaning is: 'When you don't understand something, don't pretend you know. There is no horse with all its body covered in patterns, and there is no person who knows everything.'

This Nuosu saying 'ꊈꌐꋯꑌ，ꆫꁯꇷꅑ' means 'When in-laws are united, they share a hat together.'  

Let me break it down:  
'ꊈꌐ' means 'in-law' or 'relative by marriage';  
'ꋯꑌ' means 'united' or 'together';  
'ꆫꁯ' means 'a hat';  
'ꇷ' means 'to share';  
'ꅑ' means 'to wear' or 'to put on.'  

Here's an example sentence: 'ꀎꋏꑠꃅꉉꊈꌐꋯꑌ，ꆫꁯꇷꅑꄸ，ꋯꑌꃯꉘꃅꐥ.'  
Its Chinese meaning is: 'The elders said that when in-laws are united, they share a hat together, and they should value unity.'

The Yi proverb 'ꅇꆹꀎꋏꅲ，ꋙꆹꀊꉘꍈ' translates into Chinese as '话问先辈，饭喂后代.' Its extended meaning is 'Listen to the elders.'  

Let's break it down word by word:  
'ꅇ' means 'words' or 'speech';  
'ꀒꋏ' means 'elders' or 'ancestors';  
'ꋚ' means 'food' or 'meal';  
'ꀊꉘ' means 'children.'  

For example: 'ꅇꆹꀒꋏꅲ，ꋙꆹꀊꉘꍈ，ꎔꃅꀎꋏꅇꅲ.'  
This sentence translates into Chinese as: 'Ask the elders first, feed the children later. Listen carefully to the elders.'

This saying 'ꂣꌠꉜꄡꄸ，ꂣꀱꑳꇁꇐ；ꎭꌠꉜꄡꄸ，ꎭꀱꈚꇁꇐ' can be understood as: 'Don't look down on the weak, because the weak can become strong; don't look down on the poor, because the poor can become rich.'  

Let's break it down word by word:  
'ꂣꌠ' means 'the weak';  
'ꉜꄡꄸ' means 'don't look down on';  
'ꂣꀱꑳꇁꇐ' means 'become strong';  
'ꎭꌠ' means 'the poor';  
'ꎭꀱ' means 'to become';  
'ꈚꇁꇐ' means 'become rich'.  

Example sentence:  
'ꉢꉜꇬꆹ，ꂣꌠꉜꄡꄸ，ꂣꀱꑳꇁꇐ；ꎭꌠꉜꄡꄸ，ꎭꀱꈚꇁꇐ.'  

Its Chinese meaning is: 'In my opinion, don't look down on the weak, because the weak can become strong; don't look down on the poor, because the poor can become rich.'

This Naxi saying means 'Even simple food is a meal, and even a sheepskin jacket is a piece of clothing.'  

Let me break it down:  
'ꉐꀖ' means 'mustard greens';  
'ꃱꀖ' means 'radish';  
'ꑌꋍꐚ' means 'a meal';  
'ꍶꑆ' means 'sheepskin';  
'ꇇꋩ' means 'short sleeves';  
'ꑌꋍꈬ' means 'a piece of clothing.'  

Example sentence: 'ꉐꀖꃱꀖꑌꋍꐚ，ꍶꑆꇇꋩꑌꋍꈬ，ꀃꂰꎫꋠꑌꑵꀋꐥ.'  
Its Chinese meaning is: 'Even simple food is a meal, and even a sheepskin jacket is a piece of clothing. Whatever you eat tonight is fine.'

The Yi proverb 'ꉁꆏꐋꐛ，ꌁꆏꐚꐙ' translates to 'Love makes friends, hatred makes enemies' in Chinese.  

Let's break it down word by word:  
'ꉁ' means 'love';  
'ꆏ' is a linking particle;  
'ꐋ' means 'friend';  
'ꐛ' means 'become';  
'ꌁ' means 'hate';  
'ꐚ' means 'enemy';  
'ꐙ' is also a linking particle.  

Example sentence: 'ꉁꆏꐋꐛ，ꌁꆏꐚꐙ，ꑒꑣꃅꐮꐊ.'  
This sentence translates to: 'Love makes friends, hatred makes enemies, so you should get along well.'

This saying translates to 'Ten harsh words are not as valuable as one kind word, and ten kind words are not as valuable as one important word.'  

Let's break it down:  
'ꅇꄸ' means 'harsh words';  
'ꊯꇬ' means 'ten words';  
'ꅇꂄ' means 'kind words';  
'ꀋ' is a negative marker;  
'ꁌ' means 'value' or 'worth';  
'ꅇꊨ' means 'important words'.  

For example: 'ꅇꄸꊯꇬꅇꂄꇬꀋꁌ，ꅇꂄꊯꇬꅇꈨꇬꀋꁌꄸ，ꅇꄸꏈꀋꉆ.'  
Its meaning is: 'It's said that ten harsh words are not as valuable as one kind word, and ten kind words are not as valuable as one important word. You should not speak harsh words.'

The Yi proverb 'ꉌꎔꆹꋍꍂ，ꑊꎔꆹꋍꑍ' means 'A good heart lasts a lifetime, but beauty only lasts a day.'  

Let's break it down word by word:  
'ꉌꎔ' means 'a good heart' or 'kindness';  
'ꋍꍂ' means 'a lifetime' or 'all one's life';  
'ꑊꎔ' means 'beauty' or 'looks';  
'ꋍꑍ' means 'a day' or 'for a day.'  

Example sentence: 'ꉌꎔꆹꋍꍂ，ꑊꎔꆹꋍꑍ,ꅪꁧꀉꄸꉜꀋꉆ.'  
This means: 'A good heart lasts a lifetime, but beauty only lasts a day. Don't judge by appearance.'

Sure! This Nuosu proverb — 'ꅡꈭꈍꑋꆫꌺꉬꀋꐚ，ꌳꈄꈍꑋꃆꍬꉬꀋꐚ' — translates naturally to English as: “Countless *degus* once tended sheep; countless heroes once groomed horses.”  

What’s the idea behind it? It’s a gentle but powerful reminder that no one starts out at the top — even the most respected elders (*degus*, skilled mediators and speakers) and bravest warriors began with humble, everyday jobs. Growth comes from experience, patience, and effort — not from being born into greatness.  

Breaking it down a bit:  
- ꅡꈭ means “*degu*” (a wise, eloquent mediator or traditional leader);  
- ꈍꑋ means “countless” or “innumerable”;  
- ꆫꌺ means “shepherd boy” or “child herder”;  
- ꉬ is the verb “to be”;  
- ꂳꈄ means “hero” or “brave warrior”;  
- ꃆꍬ means “horse groom” or “stable hand”.  

And here’s a real-life example sentence using it:  
'ꅡꈭꈍꑋꆫꌺꉬꀋꐚ，ꌳꈄꈍꑋꃆꍬꉬꀋꐚ，ꊋꀋꇤꆎꀋꍑꀐ.'  
That means: “Countless *degus* once tended sheep; countless heroes once groomed horses — so if you don’t put in the work, nothing will happen.”  

In short: Greatness isn’t inherited — it’s earned, step by step.

The Nuosu proverb 'ꅡꈭꂴꆽꉐꆂꈩ，ꇮꌺꂴꆽꃤꌺꈩ' means 'Speak about the law in front of a wise person, and show off your skills in front of an expert.'  

Let's break it down word by word:  
'ꅡꈭ' means 'a wise person or someone who is eloquent';  
'ꂴꆽ' means 'in front of';  
'ꉐꆂ' means 'stone';  
'ꇮꌺ' means 'an expert or craftsman';  
'ꃤꌺ' means 'skills or craftsmanship'.  

Example sentence: 'ꅡꈭꂴꆽꉐꆂꈩ，ꇮꌺꂴꆽꃤꌺꈩꄷ，ꋍꂷꋍꑵꌧ.'  
Its Chinese meaning is: 'Speak about the law in front of a wise person, and show off your skills in front of an expert. Everyone has their own area of expertise.'

The Nuosu proverb 'ꌺꐥꈄꀋꏾꂷꀋꐥ，ꅩꐥꎔꀋꏾꂷꀋꐥ' means 'Men value heroes, and women value beauty.'  

Let's look at each word one by one:  
'ꌺ' means 'men' or 'boys';  
'ꈄ' means 'hero';  
'ꅩꐥ' refers to 'girls' or 'daughters';  
'ꎔ' means 'beauty';  
'ꂷꀋꐥ' means 'without' or 'not.'  

Example sentence:  
'ꈀꄂꑌꊨꏦꈁꏾꌠꐥ，ꌺꐥꈄꀋꏾꂷꀋꐥ，ꅩꐥꎔꀋꏾꂷꀋꐥ.'  

This means: Everyone in every place has their own pursuit. Men value heroes, and women value beauty.
